<!-- [XPLAT] migrated from net48/WinForms FormKeyboard.Designer.cs — see MIGRATION_DOCS/TRANSITION_MAP.md -->
<!--
    [XPLAT] AgIO — on-screen touch keyboard dialog (Avalonia view).

    1:1 visual-parity reimplementation of the WinForms `Forms/FormKeyboard`
    (FormKeyboard.cs + FormKeyboard.Designer.cs). Per AAP §0.3.3 this faithfully reproduces the
    currently rendered Windows Forms UI — never a redesign: no new colors, no relayout, no UX
    changes. The original hosted a `keyboardString` TextBox (Tahoma 24pt Bold) above the migrated
    `Keypad.Keyboard` UserControl; this view adds the explicit OK / Cancel command buttons called
    for by the file specification (the embedded keyboard's own OK/Cancel command keys remain a
    second, equivalent dismissal path).

    MVVM CONTRACT (this view is data-bound, unlike the imperative GPS sibling):
      * DataContext is a FormKeyboardViewModel (set by the code-behind). x:DataType below enables
        Avalonia compiled bindings (AgIO.csproj sets AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault=true), so
        every {Binding} is type-checked at build time against FormKeyboardViewModel.
      * keyboardString.Text is two-way bound to FormKeyboardViewModel.EntryText — the running entry
        string the WinForms code-behind kept in keyboardString.Text. Characters raised by the
        on-screen keyboard flow through FormKeyboardViewModel.AppendKey (the original
        RegisterKeyboard1_ButtonPressed text transitions).

    WIRING CONTRACT — the partner code-behind (FormKeyboard.axaml.cs) owns dialog behaviour:
      * keyboard1.ButtonPressed (EventHandler<Keypad.KeypadKeyPressedEventArgs>) — the on-screen key
        broadcast — is subscribed in the code-behind (reproducing
        `keyboard1.ButtonPressed += RegisterKeyboard1_ButtonPressed`), NOT wired here, because it is
        a plain CLR event resolved against the x:Name="keyboard1" field.
      * btnOK closes the dialog returning the entered text (WinForms DialogResult.OK +
        ReturnString); btnCancel closes returning null (WinForms DialogResult.Cancel). Their Click
        handlers (OnOkClick / OnCancelClick) live in the code-behind — matching the sibling FormYes.
      * The named controls below MUST keep the exact x:Names `keyboardString`, `keyboard1`, `btnOK`,
        `btnCancel` to match the fields/handlers the code-behind addresses.

    WINDOW SHELL — mirrors FormKeyboard.Designer.cs:
      Text "Keyboard"                              -> Title (the code-behind may retitle to
                                                      "Enter Something", as the WinForms ctor did)
      ClientSize 940 x 460                         -> Grid MinWidth 940 under SizeToContent
      BackColor SystemColors.ActiveCaption         -> Background "#99B4D1"
      keyboard1 BackColor GradientActiveCaption    -> "#B9D1EA"
        (Windows system colors are platform-specific, so fixed literal approximations are used; they
         are theme-INDEPENDENT — correct for parity with the fixed WinForms system colors — and
         follow the App.axaml legacy parity-color convention shared with the GPS FormKeyboard view
         and the Keypad controls. AgIO is light-only, so a literal keeps the colors consistent.)
      ControlBox=false / FixedDialog / not sizable -> CanResize="False"
      (shown modally over the owner)               -> WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
      Font Tahoma                                  -> FontFamily="Tahoma" (children override sizes)

    Buttons render as TEXT (no System.Drawing, no avares imagery): the migrated AgIO.csproj
    registers no btnImages assets as <AvaloniaResource>, and every migrated AgIO view renders button
    affordances as text — consistent with the file specification and the sibling FormYes.
-->
<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:AgIO.Views"
        xmlns:keypad="clr-namespace:Keypad;assembly=Keypad"
        x:Class="AgIO.Views.FormKeyboard"
        x:DataType="vm:FormKeyboardViewModel"
        Title="Keyboard"
        SizeToContent="WidthAndHeight"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
        CanResize="False"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        SystemDecorations="Full"
        FontFamily="Tahoma"
        Background="#99B4D1">

    <!--
        Three-row layout: the entry field spans the top, the on-screen keyboard fills the body, and
        the OK / Cancel command row pins to the bottom-right. The outer Margin (5,4,9,5) aligns the
        content with the WinForms coordinates (left 5 == keyboard1.Location.X, top 4 == keyboardString
        top). MinWidth 940 reproduces the WinForms ClientSize width under SizeToContent; the keyboard
        already measures ~925 wide so this is a parity floor. The star body row sizes to the keyboard's
        content under SizeToContent (the same star-row + SizeToContent pattern proven in FormYes).
    -->
    <Grid Margin="5,4,9,5"
          RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto"
          MinWidth="940">

        <!-- keyboardString — WinForms TextBox (98,4) 751x46, Tahoma 24pt Bold, editable, TabIndex 0.
             Stretched across the top row so it spans the full dialog width. Tahoma 24pt renders at
             32 DIP (24 * 96/72), matching the identical GPS FormKeyboard entry field. Two-way bound
             to FormKeyboardViewModel.EntryText; the code-behind assigns the initial caret position. -->
        <TextBox x:Name="keyboardString"
                 Grid.Row="0"
                 Height="46"
                 HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                 VerticalAlignment="Top"
                 FontSize="32"
                 FontWeight="Bold"
                 VerticalContentAlignment="Center"
                 Text="{Binding EntryText, Mode=TwoWay}"
                 AutomationProperties.Name="Entered text" />

        <!-- keyboard1 — the migrated Keypad.Keyboard UserControl (WinForms keyboard1, Location (5,55)
             Size 930x400, BackColor GradientActiveCaption). The control has a fixed intrinsic size
             (925x525 in Keyboard.axaml) which its own code-behind shrinks at load; anchoring it
             Top|Left places it at the body-row top-left exactly like the WinForms Location, and under
             SizeToContent the window grows to fit it. ButtonPressed is subscribed in the code-behind,
             not wired here. -->
        <keypad:Keyboard x:Name="keyboard1"
                         Grid.Row="1"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         VerticalAlignment="Top"
                         Margin="0,4,0,0"
                         Background="#B9D1EA" />

        <!-- OK / Cancel command row. Cancel precedes OK so OK stays right-most (the sibling FormYes
             ordering). Touch-friendly 110x56 text buttons. btnOK is the accept button (Enter -> OK
             via IsDefault); btnCancel is the cancel button (Esc -> Cancel via IsCancel). The Click
             handlers (OnOkClick / OnCancelClick) are defined in the code-behind: OK closes returning
             the entered text, Cancel closes returning null. -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Row="2"
                    Orientation="Horizontal"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                    VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                    Spacing="12"
                    Margin="0,8,0,0">

            <Button x:Name="btnCancel"
                    Content="Cancel"
                    IsCancel="True"
                    Width="110"
                    Height="56"
                    FontSize="18"
                    HorizontalContentAlignment="Center"
                    VerticalContentAlignment="Center"
                    Click="OnCancelClick" />

            <Button x:Name="btnOK"
                    Content="OK"
                    IsDefault="True"
                    Width="110"
                    Height="56"
                    FontSize="18"
                    HorizontalContentAlignment="Center"
                    VerticalContentAlignment="Center"
                    Click="OnOkClick" />
        </StackPanel>
    </Grid>
</Window>
